{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`rebar transition <id> in_progress closed --ref origin/main` resolves the ref MORE THAN ONCE\nduring a close. If `origin/main` advances between the verification step and the signing step,\ncompletion verification PASSES against the first sha and the signer then REFUSES to attest,\nbecause the state it is asked to sign has drifted from the state that was verified.\n\nThe close SUCCEEDS. The ticket reaches `closed` with `class` recorded and no error is printed.\nWhat is silently missing is the completion-verifier attestation.\n\n## Observed\n\nClosing `cutprice-untameable-stork` (326f-595a-088b-40a5):\n\n- verification ran against `6fe5c0d54574`\n- `origin/main` advanced to `d44bd253ee44` mid-run\n- verification PASSED; the signer refused the drifted state\n- result: `status=closed`, `close_class=preexisting`, **no completion-verifier signature**\n\nNothing in the output flags the gap. It is only visible if you go looking for the attestation.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThe attestation is the durable evidence that a close was earned rather than asserted. A close\nthat passes verification but silently loses its signature is indistinguishable, after the fact,\nfrom one that was never verified — which is precisely the property the gate exists to provide.\n\nThe window is not narrow in practice: a completion-verified close takes 9-11 minutes, and on a\nrepo with active CI and multiple agents `origin/main` moves several times an hour. This session\nalone moved main a dozen times.\n\n## Mechanism (hypothesis — not yet proven)\n\nTwo independent resolutions of the same symbolic ref, with a multi-minute LLM verification\nbetween them. A single resolve-once-then-reuse would remove the race. I have NOT read the close\npath to confirm where the second resolution happens; whoever picks this up should.\n\n## Workaround, and why it is not a fix\n\nPass an immutable sha:\n\n    SHA=$(git rev-parse origin/main)\n    rebar transition <id> in_progress closed --class <c> --ref \"$SHA\"\n\nThat works, but it requires every caller to know the hazard. The documented guidance and the\ntool's own help present `--ref origin/main` as ordinary usage, and the CLI close gate's DEFAULT\n(worktree HEAD) has the same shape — a HEAD that moves mid-run would race identically.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] A symbolic `--ref` is resolved ONCE at the close boundary and the resolved sha REPLACES\n      the argument, so verification, snapshot materialisation, the drift guard and signing all\n      see the same immutable commit (`_pin_completion_ref`, `transition_close.py:135`, rebound\n      by the caller at `:618`).\n- [x] If the verified and signing shas ever disagree, the close emits a loud stderr warning\n      naming BOTH shas and the exact remediation, and refuses to attest stale state\n      (`transition_close.py:402-411`).\n      OPERATOR DECISION 2026-08-28: a warning is SUFFICIENT. The close is deliberately NOT\n      required to exit non-zero, because that would change the contract for every existing\n      caller (scripts, the library facade, and the MCP surface all expect exit 0 today). The\n      original wording asked the close to FAIL; it warns and succeeds, and that is accepted.\n      Note the drift branch is now near-unreachable: the pin removes the symbolic-ref split,\n      and 4de6 already pinned the default HEAD target.\n- [x] A close that cannot obtain an attestation says so on stderr, naming the cause and how to\n      certify. The machine-readable `completion_signature` marker is already present on the\n      library, JSON and MCP surfaces. The residual — the default CLI *text* output on stdout\n      not inspecting that marker — is tracked separately as `faulty-floppy-kob`\n      (`4c28-0883-bf98-424b`), filed from this investigation.\n- [x] A regression test drives a close with `--ref` advancing MID-RUN and asserts the close\n      lands SIGNED and bound to the verified tree\n      (`tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_close_symbolic_ref_pins_3e21.py`); seeding the defect\n      back returns it to RED.\n\n## Found by\n\nA close performed during this session; the missing signature was noticed only because the\nclosing agent checked for it explicitly. Recorded because the failure is silent by construction."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787900010029697001,"uuid":"922cb3f5-4db3-4680-a14a-fdbd7a0d7b2a"}